1
00:00:00,604 --> 00:00:14,047
Every AI answer uses water, but nobody can see how much. Dropwise sits in front of the Bifrost AI gateway, estimates the water behind every request, and cuts the waste.

2
00:00:14,399 --> 00:00:20,886
Apps point at Dropwise instead of Bifrost, and nothing else in their code changes.

3
00:00:24,904 --> 00:00:30,956
This is the live Requests page. I'll send real requests through the gateway.

4
00:00:31,308 --> 00:00:39,099
An easy prompt, asked of the large Opus model. Dropwise routes it to the small Haiku model.

5
00:00:40,453 --> 00:00:46,940
Asking again is answered from the cache: no model call, and no new water.

6
00:00:48,292 --> 00:00:55,214
A time-sensitive question skips the cache on purpose, so it never serves a stale answer.

7
00:00:56,565 --> 00:01:01,748
A hard prompt stays on Opus, so quality isn't traded away.

8
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:17,952
Every request gets a receipt: tokens measured, and water estimated as a range.

9
00:01:23,238 --> 00:01:31,899
The receipt names the exact registry version behind each factor, so every number can be traced to its source.

10
00:01:35,791 --> 00:01:45,756
The Overview now shows what just happened. Measured numbers are labelled measured. Water is always an estimated range, with what it counts.

11
00:01:50,213 --> 00:02:00,178
Teams get daily water budgets, with an alert at eighty percent. The limit is soft: a team is flagged, but still served.

12
00:02:04,039 --> 00:02:14,439
Before and after: the same two hundred requests, with and without Dropwise. About twenty-two percent fewer model calls, and twenty-four percent fewer tokens.

13
00:02:14,791 --> 00:02:22,582
These answers are replayed recordings, so the cost shown is a what-if, and the page says so.

14
00:02:26,453 --> 00:02:44,244
Routing and cache. The classifier is plain rules, with no model call, so it adds almost no delay. Only prompts it is confident are easy go to the small model; a hard one like this stays on the large model.

15
00:02:48,113 --> 00:02:54,165
A request can be deferred. I'll send one with a six hour deadline.

16
00:02:57,782 --> 00:03:11,225
Right now is already the lowest-demand hour in the profile, so Dropwise runs it at once, and says why. At a busier hour it would wait for the quiet one.

17
00:03:11,576 --> 00:03:21,106
The hourly profile is illustrative and says so. The water estimate doesn't change with the hour, so we claim no saving.

18
00:03:24,966 --> 00:03:37,540
The claim checker splits a claim into parts and checks each against the registry: supported, overstated, understated, unsupported or outdated, with citations. This one uses a saved example.

19
00:03:41,400 --> 00:03:46,583
Every factor has a source, a date and a signed version.

20
00:03:53,403 --> 00:04:08,586
Tavily finds newer sources, Haiku reads the figure, and code checks the quote is really in the source before an old figure is flagged stale. Nothing enters the registry until a person accepts it.

21
00:04:11,947 --> 00:04:22,347
Dropwise: measured where it can be, estimated where it must be, and honest about which is which. It is open source. Thank you.

